fsetting the district's five-point Republican lean", "Open seat after Chuck Edwards withdrew, removing incumbent advantage", "Heavy Republican counterinvestment, including Vance rallies in Hendersonville and Helene relief messaging"], "rationale": "Ager's massive money advantage, a favorable national environment, and the open seat outweigh the district's Republican lean, and prediction markets price him well ahead even though most raters still call this a toss-up. The single available poll, from a Democratic-leaning firm, shows only a narrow lead, so the edge stays modest.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a late surge of Republican outside spending and surrogate visits for Balkcom could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 11th District is an open seat after Republican Chuck Edwards withdrew, and the contest between Democrat Jamie Ager and Republican Jennifer Balkcom has become one of the cycle's most competitive House races. The district leans Republican by about five points, but a strong national environment for Democrats this year offsets that tilt. Ager has raised roughly fifteen times more money than Balkcom and holds a large cash advantage, while outside spending so far has backed him.\n\nOnly one public poll, taken in September by a Democratic-leaning firm, shows Ager narrowly ahead. Most race raters call the seat a toss-up, though two rate it a lean toward Democrats and prediction markets price Ager's chances near four in five. Vice President Vance's repeated visits to Hendersonville underline how hard Republicans are working to hold the seat. The forecast gives Ager a modest edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5972, "margin": 1.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6249, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6236, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6239, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0